=== V. The End Times
--- The End Times: Rapture, Millennium, Eschatology
lead: **Eschatology**: the doctrines and teachings of the end times.
panel: Rapture | Latin for “caught up,” a theological term for the resurrection of believers on the day of redemption, the gathering of the elect. The exact timing of this event is disputed by differing interpretations of Scripture surrounding it, but the reality of it is indisputable. [[Matthew 24:31]] [[1 Thessalonians 4:13-18]]
panel: Millennium | The period of time between the end of the wrath, where Satan is bound for 1,000 years in Hell, and his release to deceive the nations for “a little season.” This view is called Premillennialism by those holding a “postmillennial” or “amillennial” position. [[Revelation 20:1-3]]
panel: Eschatology | The doctrines and teachings of the End Times.

=== VI. Rightly Dividing the Word
--- Rightly Dividing the Word
lead: Two opposite approaches to Scripture: one starts with the text, the other starts with a conclusion already in hand.
panel: Exegesis | Receiving doctrine from the interpretation or understanding of Scripture. “The Bible says it, I believe it.” You start with the text and let it speak.
panel-warn: Eisegesis | Using the text to justify existing traditions or opinions, and to confirm biases. You start with a conclusion and go looking for a verse to support it. Watch for it: “Faith without works is dead” ([[James 2:20]]); “Baptism doth now save us” ([[1 Peter 3:21]]); “Make Jesus Lord of your life”; “Repent of your sin to be saved.” Context matters: God is not the author of confusion ([[1 Corinthians 14:33]]), and it is not up to you to square a contradiction for the person who introduced it. It is on them to.

--- A Simple Word Study
image: lessons/doctrine/doctrine-week-6-terms-and-conditions-12.jpg | The word sin and its variations appear approximately 447 times in the KJV New Testament | A Simple Word Study: an Example of Careful Exegesis
note: Count check against the KJV text: “sin” appears 447 times in the whole Bible (112 times in the New Testament). In the New Testament, sin, sins, sinned and sinneth together appear 222 times.
